# OIDC identity provider config for the SPA (Authorization Code + PKCE via
# react-oidc-context / oidc-client-ts). Generic/OIDC-standard — swapping the
# IdP (Zitadel locally, Keycloak, etc.) is a config change only.
#
# Zitadel serves discovery at the instance root, so the authority is the
# bare origin — no per-provider path.
VITE_OIDC_AUTHORITY=http://localhost:8080
VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/
VITE_OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/
VITE_OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email

# Optional link behind the sidebar's "Account settings" item. There is no
# local user table, so profile edits and password changes happen in the IdP's
# own self-service page, opened in a new tab — e.g. Keycloak's account
# console:
#   VITE_ACCOUNT_URL=http://localhost:8080/realms/fabric-sync/account/
# Unset, and Authentik's `/application/o/<slug>/` authority layout resolves
# to its user interface (`{base}/if/user/`) automatically; anything else
# hides the menu item. What the page offers (editable profile fields, the
# password-change button, MFA) follows the brand's user-settings flow in the
# Authentik admin UI.

# Same-origin API via the dev-server proxy (`/api` -> BACKEND_URL in
# vite.config.ts, default http://localhost:8000). Keeps the frontend and
# backend on one origin so there are no CORS preflights.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api
# ...or bypass the proxy and hit the backend directly instead (the backend's
# CORS layer is permissive, so this works too):
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Optional: point the dev-server API proxy at a backend on a different
# origin/port (read by vite.config.ts from the process env, not baked in).
# BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:8000
